WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.100
What’s the best way
to stay healthy

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.620
in the face of so much
conflicting nutrition information?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.580
Well, ideally, you would
go to the source.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.480
The gold standard;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.100
the peer-reviewed
medical literature.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.800
Read through stacks of
the latest medical journals.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.900
But who’s got
time for that?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.040
I do!

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.620
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.200
Today we look at how
adding a healthy option

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.680
can actually drive people to
make even worse choices,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.100
thanks to a mind-blowing
glitch of human psychology.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.200
In 2017, and to much fanfare,
menu labeling for calorie counts

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.840
began to be mandated
in national chain restaurants.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.660
I mean shouldn't consumers
have the knowledge needed

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.820
to make healthy eating
choices outside the home?

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.300
It just makes sense that caloric
information on menus will help

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.840
consumers limit food intake
to stay within daily energy needs,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.840
but it didn't work. It turns out
calorie labels are not effective,

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.220
perhaps shaving on average an
insignificant 8 calories off of a meal.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.360
You could have totally
predicted that. Why?

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.220
Just as one might divine the value
of front-of-pack traffic light labeling

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.340
from the ferocity of the food
industry response against it,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.000
one could probably gauge
the futility of calorie labeling

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.060
by the ease at which some
regulations have been passed.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.920
McDonald's voluntarily started
publishing calorie info

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.980
nationally back in 2012
after a labeling mandate

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:29.820
in New York City was found

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.700
to have no overall effect
on consumer behavior.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.300
So studies suggest such labeling
could boost "perceptions of

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.640
the restaurant's concern
for consumers' well-being"

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.540
while carefully not undermining
any Big Mac attacks.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.780
At the same time,
McDonald's announced plans

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.180
for adding seasonal
produce to their menu.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.800
How cynical do you have to be not to
at least recognize that as a good thing?

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.200
Ironically, adding a healthy option
can actually drive people

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.240
to make even worse choices.
Ready to have your mind blown?

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.900
If you offer people a choice of side
dishes—something unhealthy

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.720
like French fries or something more
neutral like a baked potato—

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.820
only about 10% of individuals with high
self-control will splurge for the fries.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.780
Good for them.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.820
French fries are so unhealthy though
that as a public health do-gooder

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.720
you add a third option, an even
healthier option than the baked potato—

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.140
a side salad to appeal
to their better natures.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.260
So then, instead of choosing
between an indulgent choice

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.380
and the more neutral baked potato,
they have their pick of

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.640
the indulgent choice, the neutral
choice, or an even healthier choice.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.300
Even if everyone doesn't choose
the salad, more will go for

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.180
the middle-ground baked potato
over the fries, right?

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.380
So how much farther does
French fry fancying fall

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:56.340
by adding the salad option to the
mix? It shoots up, tripling to 33%.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.060
Without the salad option,
only 1 in 10 chose the fries,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.720
but that jumped to a third of people
just at the sight of salad.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.840
The same thing happens when
you offer people the choice between

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.960
a bacon cheeseburger, a chicken
sandwich, or a veggie burger.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.380
In a "No Healthy Option"
scenario where people were

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.440
offered the cheeseburger, a chicken
sandwich, or a fish sandwich,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.540
17% chose the burger.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.840
Swap out the fish sandwich
for a veggie burger

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.620
and bacon cheeseburger
preference doubled to 37%.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.960
How can just seeing a healthy option push
people to make unhealthier choices?

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.980
The paper described in these series
of experiments was entitled

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.720
"Vicarious Goal Fulfillment: When the
Mere Presence of a Healthy Option

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.820
Leads to an Ironically
Indulgent Decision."

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.940
The thought is that seeing
the salad or veggie burger,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:49.680
people make the mental note to choose
that at some nebulous next time,

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.220
thereby giving them the
excuse to indulge now.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.980
See, there's a fascinating glitch of
human psychology called self-licensing.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.780
This is when we unwittingly justify
doing something that draws us away

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.080
from our goals after we've just done
something that brings us towards them,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.880
like justifying eating a donut because
you lost so much weight last week.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.620
We reward ourselves with an
indulgence that sets us back.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.100
If you give smokers
" vitamin C supplements,"

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.380
they subsequently
smoke more cigarettes

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.680
than if you give smokers what
you explain are "placebo" pills,

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.560
even though both groups were
given identical sugar pills.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.280
The group who thought they were
taking supplements smoked

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.480
nearly twice as much, perhaps
thinking at some subconscious level

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.060
that since they had just done
something good for their health,

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.340
they could afford to "live a little,"

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.980
which may have in effect indeed
occasioned them to live a little less.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.720
You can see how this could
translate into our lifestyle arenas.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.260
Those given placebo pills they
believed to be dietary supplements

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.900
not only expressed less desire to
subsequently engage in exercise,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.840
but followed through by
walking about a third less.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.080
Compared to those who were
told the pills were placebos,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.680
misled participants were also
more likely to choose a buffet

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.320
over what was described
as a "healthy, organic meal."

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.880
Would they eat more too?

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.740
A seminal study entitled

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.680
"The liberating effect of weight loss
supplements on dietary control"

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.600
put it to the test.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.720
Participants were randomized
to take a known placebo

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.380
or a purported weight loss supplement—
actually just the same placebo—

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.380
and later covertly observed at a buffet.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.560
Not only did the supplement
subjects eat more food,

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.300
they chose less healthy items.
They also ate about 30% more candy

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.660
in a bogus "taste test" and
ordered more sugary drinks.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.380
Hence, the investigators
concluded, "people who rely

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.120
on dietary supplements
for health protection

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.280
may pay a hidden price: the curse
of licensed self-indulgence."

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:56.760
So, circling back, what the vicarious
goal fulfillment studies added is that

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.540
not only does making progress towards
a goal rationalize decision making

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.380
that undermines us, but even just
considering making progress

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.180
can have a similar licensing effect.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.120
Note the study subjects were
not only moved to make

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.960
the unhealthier choice,
but the unhealthiest choice.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.740
You'd think that even if people didn't
go for the salad or veggie option,

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.740
the presence of a healthier alternative
might at least have encouraged them

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.700
to choose something in-between: not
as healthy, but not the unhealthiest,

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.640
but instead it moved people
in the opposite direction.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.380
Compared to the "No Healthy Option"
of chocolate-covered Oreos,

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:36.840
regular Oreos, or golden Oreos,
adding a "lower-calorie" Oreo option

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.560
doubled the likelihood that the study
participants would go straight

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.960
for the most indulgent
chocolate-covered option.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.700
This is attributed to another illogical
quirk of human psychology

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.820
indelicately named the
"what the hell effect."

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.980
This is when one forbidden cookie
can lead dieters to eat the whole bag.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.960
Once you've already strayed
from your goals,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.940
well then, why not go all the way?

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.540
So once people decide they're
going to get that salad next time

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:07.080
and spoil themselves just this once,
they might as well go for

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.460
the most indulgent choice.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.660
The halo of healthy foods
can even warp our perceptions.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.540
Show weight-conscious people
a burger and nothing else,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.900
then ask them to estimate the number
of calories, and the average answer

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.140
is 734 calories.
Now show folks

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.680
the exact same burger
accompanied by 3 celery sticks,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.340
and they guess the total
comes out to 619 calories.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.740
Did they think the celery
had negative calories?

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.620
No, most knew the
celery had calories, too,

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.860
but just the juxtaposition
of the burger with the celery

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.180
made the burger seem healthier.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.220
The same thing happens
when you add an apple

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.440
to a bacon-and-cheese
waffle sandwich,

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.500
a side salad to beef chili, or some
carrots next to a cheese steak.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.780
About a hundred calories
appear to disappear.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.660
Health halo effects may explain
why people are more likely

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.880
to order a dessert and
more sugary drinks

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:58.140
with a "healthier" sub at Subway
versus a Big Mac at McDonald's,

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.000
even though the sub used in the study—
filled with ham, salami, and pepperoni—

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.460
had 50% more calories to begin with.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.520
Even just a reference
to healthy foods can do it.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.720
Show people a picture of a Big Mac and
people estimate it has 646 calories.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.480
Okay, then just add the words

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.780
"For your health, eat at least five
fruits and vegetables per day,"

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.040
and all the sudden, the same burger
in the same ad was thought

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.900
to only have 503 calories.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.920
Merely offering or even
promoting salads and fruit

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:32.640
can bring McDonald's accolades
and bolster consumer loyalty

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.892
without, ironically,
helping their health.

00:08:36.306 --> 00:08:37.980
In our next story,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.680
we look at how mandating
healthy eating messaging

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.347
on fast-food ads ironically
only makes things worse.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.020
Denmark ended up canceling
its saturated fat tax

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.600
on meat and dairy and
shelving their sugar tax,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.840
because the farming and
food company interests claimed

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.660
too many jobs would be
lost if people ate healthier.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.080
Apparently, a healthy
economy was more important

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.880
than a healthy citizenry.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.940
Ironically, the fat
tax was abolished

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.640
just when evidence of
the effects started to appear.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.700
The intake of saturated
fat among Danish consumers

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.280
from some meat and
dairy products was declining,

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:16.020
but not from sour cream.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.840
The public ended up eating so
much more low-fat sour cream

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.840
that it ended up outweighing
the smaller reduction

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.100
in high-fat sour cream.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.280
So, you always have to think
about the unintended consequences.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.600
If incentives have people swapping
out sugary cookies for salty chips,

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.020
for example, it might not be doing
the public’s health many favors.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.960
One field study of
a tax on soda found

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.700
that you can drop
soft drink purchases,

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.060
but households may just
end up buying more beer.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.020
Another study found that
calorie labeling of sugary drinks

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.720
ironically led to an
increase in consumption,

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.700
presumed to be
because the consumers

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.640
may have previously overestimated
their caloric content.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.320
Stark warnings about the risks of
unintended negative consequences

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.320
of obesity-targeted health
policies are trumpeted by those

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.280
with ties to the likes of Coca-Cola,
Kraft, PepsiCo, Wrigley,

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:07.350
Red Bull, the World
Sugar Research Organization,

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:09.960
the National Cattlemen’s
Association, Mars,

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.200
and corn syrup giant
Archer Daniels Midland

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.140
(and that’s just
a single scientist).

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:20.520
The concern about unintended
consequences shouldn’t paralyze

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.040
our efforts, but should
serve up a healthy dose

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.760
of humility when considering
policy proposals.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.300
How about releasing a video game
for kids that promotes fruit?

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:33.900
Sounds good, right? Well,
what do you think happened

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.600
when kids were sat down in
front of bowls of fruit and candy

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:40.260
and randomized to play one of
three different computer “advergames”

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.860
(advertising-game hybrids
incorporating product placements)

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.920
promoting either candy,
fruit, or toys?

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:51.600
The pro-candy game group ate
more candy, but disappointingly

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.820
the pro-fruit group
didn’t eat more fruit.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.800
But then it gets interesting.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.920
They ate more candy
in the fruit game, too.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.380
Compared to the toy
promotion control group,

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.780
having a kid play a
video game promoting fruit

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:05.940
led them to eat more candy.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.640
Presumably, both the
candy and fruit games

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:09.900
just made them think about food,

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.140
and they naturally gravitated
to their preferred snack.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.620
One of the most fascinating
phenomena I came across

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.500
was the boomerang effect
of “remedy messaging.”

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.580
One might presume that the
advertising of smoking cessation aids

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.520
like nicotine gum would
help make quitting easier.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.240
After all, the vast majority
of smokers want to quit and so

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.600
availing them of helpful options
couldn’t help but help, right?

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:37.560
Instead, such remedy
marketing can create a vicarious

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.120
“get out of jail free card”

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.640
that ends up reinforcing
risky behavior.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:44.880
Exposure to nicotine
replacement product advertising

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.700
was found to undermine
quitting intentions,

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.800
especially among the
heaviest smokers,

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.900
the very ones who
needed it the most.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:56.640
The thought is that smokers may
subconsciously interpret the remedy

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.240
as evidence that the hazards
of smoking are more manageable,

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.560
hence less risky, and thereby
help to justify their habit.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.820
You can see how easily this would
translate to the weight loss arena.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:12.480
Previously, I explored how
self-licensing could cause

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.320
those taking slimming supplements
to inadvertently eat more,

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.820
but just exposure to a
“fat-fighting pill” advertisement

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:22.860
appeared to have a
similar type of effect.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.280
So, even when companies are
ostensibly selling health

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.980
rather than disease,
they still may be inadvertently

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:30.600
making the problem worse.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:33.180
In the marketplace,
there’s just no incentive

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.860
for risk avoidance messaging.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:37.980
Nobody makes money selling
“just say no” unless

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:40.920
it can be somehow linked to
saleable products and services.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:45.900
A policy in France—where
burgers now outsell baguettes—

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.260
may represent an
interesting real-world example

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:52.920
of this counterintuitive
remedy messaging effect.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.400
Industry lobbyists
morphed a valiant effort

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.220
to ban the advertising of
junk into a mandate for

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:02.640
preventive health messaging
on junk food advertisements.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:06.900
So, now on products like
“Lay's Chips Saveur Poulet Rôti”

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:08.940
(chicken-flavored potato chips),

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:10.680
you’ll see messages like

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:13.680
“Pour votre santé, pratiquez une
activité physique régulière,”

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:16.620
"For your health, practice
regular physical activity."

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:18.420
Sounds good, right?

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.960
But any time an industry
agrees to a regulation,

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:25.200
one should get skeptical
as to its effectiveness.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:29.640
To see if such messaging might
lead to a boomerang effect,

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.940
research subjects were randomized
to view a Big Mac advertisement

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.340
with or without the
preventive health message

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:37.320
“Eat five fruits and
vegetables a day.”

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.900
I mean, wouldn’t it
be great if McDonald’s

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:41.820
was forced to
advertise healthy food?

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:45.240
The participants then filled
out some BS questionnaire

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:47.220
and before they left
were allowed to choose

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.460
one of two McDonald’s coupons as
a reward for their participation:

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.220
a free sundae or a
free bag of fruit.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:56.460
Guess who was more
likely to pick the fruit?

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.580
Only one-in-three of
those who just saw

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:02.040
the straight burger advertisement
chose the fruit over the sundae,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.160
but that number fell
to only one-in-six

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.320
among those who’d been
prompted to eat healthier.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.040
Let that sink in for a second.
Isn’t that crazy?

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.860
The absence of the
healthy message

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.020
doubled the choice
of the healthy snack.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.660
The health message
made things worse.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:22.800
This may be the remedy messaging
boomerang effect in action.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:27.480
By simultaneously offering a
temptation with a reminder about

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.640
how they can dig
themselves out later

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:32.880
justifies the excuse to indulge.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.900
Subconsciously, it may give
the chicken-y chip eater

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:40.320
the rationalization that they
can just work it off the next day,

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.800
even if the next day
at the gym never comes.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:48.480
The advised antidote to
avoid justification effects

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.400
is to instead use negative framing.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.760
Instead of offering a way out
to compensate for indulging

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.020
“just this one time,” cautionary
messages may be more effective.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:02.160
For example, on your
next ham and cheese or

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.280
chocolate-filled
croissant, a message like:

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.440
“For your health,
avoid foods that are

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:09.420
too fatty, too sweet,
or too salty.”

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.480
That’s a message for
which I doubt Le McDonald’s

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.120
would be as enthusiastic.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.800
Learning about this rebound
effect has made me reflect about

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:24.060
my work on NutritionFacts.org,
distilled into How Not to Die.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.900
It doesn’t get much more
remedy messaging than that!

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:31.440
If we can get such dramatic benefits
with diet and lifestyle changes

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.200
so late in life, in such
short amount of time,

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:37.860
why can’t we just live
lives of gluttony and sloth

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:40.920
and then around 50 or so,
just clean up our acts?

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.800
The reason is because
it may be too late.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.800
Our first symptom
may be our last.

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:50.400
Most people know the number one
killer of both men and women

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.380
in the United States
is heart disease.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.500
What people may not
know, though, is that,

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.000
according to the American
Heart Association,

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:00.060
for the majority of Americans
that die of heart disease,

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:03.540
their first symptom occurs
not years before they die,

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:06.900
but literally minutes
before they die.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:11.760
Sudden cardiac death is the first
manifestation of heart disease

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.400
for the majority of individuals,
particularly among women,

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:17.460
meaning that you had no idea
you even had heart disease

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:20.040
until you’re literally
dying from it.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.920
Heart disease reversal is great,

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.500
but that’s why
prevention is the key.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.840
When it comes to sudden death,
an ounce of prevention

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.180
truly is worth more
than a pound of cure,

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:33.993
because there is no cure, for dead.

